    "body": "> It was just to illustrate the behavior that during the parent's initial focus steps, the child popover element doesn't mess with the initial focus even if it has the autofocus attribute.\r\n\r\nI think it *should* mess with the initial focus. You put the autofocus attribute on it! That's explicitly asking it to mess with the initial focus; that's what autofocus means!\r\n\r\n> Do you think this is a use case we should or shouldn't support?\r\n\r\nI still can't tell what the actual use case is. Why would someone write code like this? What is motivating this change? I'm specifically interested in some example code that represents something a real app developer would do, i.e. a real user experience they're trying to create which the current spec prevents. I think the example you gave above is not one of those.",
